---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import SiteCheckForm from "../../components/SiteCheckForm.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Stops right-click copying in the browser", them: "Easily bypassed", us: "-" },
  { label: "Stops scrapers and feed copying", them: "No", us: "-" },
  { label: "Protects your text once it leaves your store", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Proves a stolen description was yours", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Produces evidence for a DMCA takedown", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Copy blockers vs Kirpik"
  description="A plain comparison of copy and right-click blockers with Kirpik: what a storefront blocking script does, whether it prevents content theft, and where proof of ownership comes from."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Copy blockers vs Kirpik"
    lead="Most apps in Shopify's anti-theft category run a script in the visitor's browser that cancels right-click, text selection, copying or keyboard shortcuts. This page covers the technique itself, across every app that uses it."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What a copy blocker is</h2>
      <p>
        Install one of these apps and a small script joins your theme. While a page is open, the script
        cancels selection and clipboard events, hides the context menu, swallows keyboard shortcuts, and
        sometimes watches for the developer tools. It has no server component and it changes nothing
        about the text itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some listings say this themselves. CopyBlock offers to "discourage casual copying on your
        storefront". Defendify calls itself "an extra layer of deterrence against casual content theft".
        Cozy AntiTheft says it "makes it harder to steal content". The developer of Content Protection by
        Webyze wrote in a public reply on its own listing: "there is no way to protect the content 100%,
        just ways to add hurdles." Even the hurdles do not hold: reader mode, the page source or
        switching JavaScript off bypasses the script in seconds, and browsers keep those controls with
        the user.
      </p>

      <h2>Does it prevent content theft?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No.</strong> A copy blocker relies on a script in the visitor's browser, and a storefront
        script does not prevent content theft. It does not stop Shopify serving your content through the
        machine-readable feeds (JSON, XML and ATOM) that scrapers actually read, it is easily bypassed
        and easily disabled, and the friction it creates lands on legitimate customers rather than on
        content thieves.
      </p>
      <p>
        A scraper or dropship importer reads those feeds directly. It never encounters the blocker,
        because the script exists only on pages that load it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik does not claim to prevent content theft: it <strong>protects</strong> content. The
        watermark travels inside the text itself, so stolen content stays identifiable and provable
        wherever it surfaces. Kirpik adds no scripts to your storefront, so it runs alongside any
        blocker without conflict.
      </p>

      <h2>Can it prove infringement?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No.</strong> A blocking script leaves no trace in the text and keeps no record of what
        was taken, so when your description turns up on another site it offers nothing to show whose
        text it was.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik proves infringement through the watermark and the Proof of Ownership Certificate.
        Detection reads your watermark out of the stolen text, and the certificate records dated
        fingerprints, the infringing page's timestamps, Wayback Machine corroboration and a public
        verification page, ready for a DMCA takedown.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="tablehead">Copy blockers vs Kirpik</h2>
      <CompareTable themLabel="Copy blockers" rows={rows} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <SiteCheckForm tone="band" heading="Is your store's content protected?" cta="Check my store">
    <Fragment slot="sub">
      Do you use <strong>a right-click blocker</strong> or any other "Content Protection" App? Enter
      your Shopify store address to see if your content is actually protected.
    </Fragment>
  </SiteCheckForm>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <section class="fineprint">
        <p>
          This page describes the storefront-blocker technique across the Shopify anti-theft category.
          Quotes are from the named apps' own public Shopify App Store listings as recorded in June and
          July 2026; listings change. The limitations discussed are general properties of browsers and
          platforms rather than defects of any app. Each named app has its own page in
          <a href="/compare/">the full comparison survey</a>. App names and trademarks belong to their
          respective owners and are used for identification only. Nothing here is legal advice.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tablehead { font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 1.15rem + 1.2vw, 2rem); }
  .fineprint { padding-top: 1.4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .fineprint p { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 78ch; }
  .fineprint a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
